  <revision rev="2" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>b243bcdf6d90e1f7fda5088326d98b72</srcmd5>
    <version>0.1.1</version>
    <time>1787579028</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update python-comfy-angle to 0.1.1.

Upstream 0.1.1 leaves the import API unchanged (get_lib_dir, get_egl_path,
get_glesv2_path); its only source change is a docstring. The substantive work
is upstream's own: Linux ANGLE libraries are now built from the pinned ANGLE
revision without X11/Wayland/GBM instead of being extracted from Electron,
plus a new Windows arm64 wheel. This package ships none of those prebuilt
binaries - it stays the Mesa/libglvnd-backed distro implementation that
locates the system libEGL.so.1 / libGLESv2.so.2 - so only the version, the
docs and the prose that still said &quot;Electron-extracted&quot; needed updating.
%check additionally asserts the installed dist metadata version equals
%{version}, so the spec and the hand-maintained pyproject.toml cannot drift.

License stays MIT: every shipped file is distro-authored, nothing is vendored;
upstream's BSD-3-Clause covers only the ANGLE binaries we do not ship. No
patches, no _service, no shared library.

Built natively on aarch64 against openSUSE:Factory:ARM/standard with --clean:
rpmlint 0 errors / 0 warnings / 0 badness, %check ran on python313 and
python314 and ctypes-loaded the real system libEGL/libGLESv2 in the chroot.
spec-cleaner no-diff, source_validator rc0.</comment>
    <requestid>1373166</requestid>
  </revision>
</revisionlist>
